
What It Actually Does


Live Triage (-t)

It sweeps the system fast to see who is on the machine and how they are trying to stay there.
• Pulls History Logs: Looks up every user on the box and grabs their last 20 terminal commands from .bash_history or .zsh_history.
• Audits Cron Jobs: Checks /etc/crontab, /etc/cron.d, and user crontabs to find active scheduled tasks while ignoring blank space and comment lines.

Ghost Process Resurrection (Runs with -t)

This is the "Lay on Hands" module for catching malware that deletes itself right after starting.
• Finds Deleted Binaries: Crawls through /proc to find any process running from an unlinked path marked as (deleted).
• Sniffs the Command Line: Unpacks the arguments from /proc/[PID]/cmdline, strips out the messy null bytes, and prints exactly what command started it.
• Pulls Files from the Void: Clones the executing binary directly out of memory and saves it as a healthy .bin file before the process can close.

Process Memory Dumper (-m [PID])

Rips the virtual memory straight out of a running program to catch hidden configurations or plain-text strings.
• Maps out Memory: Reads /proc/[PID]/maps to find where the heap, stack, and readable segments are hiding.
• Dumps Raw Data: Streams /proc/[PID]/mem, strips out empty zeroed blocks to save space, and dumps out the raw segments labeled by their original system name.

Raw Drive Carver (-c)

Sweeps raw storage streams or whole drives (like /dev/sda) to salvage deleted files based on their bytes.
• Sliding-Window Stream: Reads files in massive 4MB chunks, passing lingering data down a queue so files split across sectors don't get clipped.
• Extension-Agnostic Hunting: Looks at raw magic bytes, completely ignoring the file name. It tracks down ELF, PNG, JPEG, PDF, ZIP, and scripts (#!). It explicitly flags Windows MZ binaries as suspicious on Linux.
• Slices by the Footer: Uses precise markers (like %%EOF for PDFs) to cut the file exactly where it ends.

Deep File Dissector (-a and -r)

An on-demand file inspection dashboard that bypasses filename tricks.
• Calculates Entropy: Scores a file's data density up to 8.0 to instantly flag if a file is packed, encrypted, or compressed.
• Grabs Hashes & Metadata: Runs a 64KB block loop for SHA-256 signatures and pulls permissions, ownership, and inodes.
• Checks Low-Level Flags: Uses kernel ioctl and xattr calls to expose hidden filesystem attributes like the Immutable (+i) and Append-only (+a) modifiers.

The Command Line Switches

• target: The path to a file, directory, or raw block device (like /dev/sdb).
• -h: Standard help screen.
• -a: Turns on advanced mode to print deep filesystem stats and attributes.
• -r: Crawls folders recursively when target is a directory.
• -c: Starts up the raw byte carver on your target device.
• -m [PID]: Targets a single Process ID and dumps its memory segments.
• -t: Performs the live system triage check and runs the ghost binary resurrection engine.
• -o: Sets your custom output folder (Defaults to forensic_output).




